{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And you shall rejoice in your festival.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״ושמחת בחגך״ — and not in the joy of your wife; from this they derived that one does not mix one joy with another. Now, in the case of the festival of Sukkot Scripture mentioned both the language of rejoicing and the language of blessing: the language of rejoicing, as it says, ״ושמחת בחגך״; the language of blessing, as it is written, ״כי יברכך ה׳ אלהיך״. And so we find that for the festival of Shavuot Scripture mentioned the language of rejoicing, as it says, ״ושמחת לפני ה׳ אלהיך״, and the language of blessing, ״כאשר יברכך ה׳ אלהיך״. But for the festival of Pesach it mentioned neither the language of rejoicing nor the language of blessing. The reason is that in the two festivals, which are Shavuot and Sukkot, grain is available to householders and they are able to provide for the four guests it mentions, and since he provides for them from it, there is rejoicing and there is blessing — the guests rejoice and the householders are blessed. But on Pesach the new grain has not yet been harvested and the old has already run out, and it is not possible to bring them joy on Pesach — since the Torah descended to the end of a person's means, that he would not be able to provide for them — therefore no language of rejoicing or blessing was mentioned for it. And they gave yet another reason in the Midrash: Why did the Torah not mention rejoicing on Pesach, but mentioned rejoicing on Atzeret, and on Sukkot three rejoicings — ״ושמחת בחגך״, ״והיית אך שמח״, and ״ושמחת לפני ה׳ אלהיך״? Because we learned: On four occasions the world is judged: on Pesach, concerning the grain; on Atzeret, concerning the fruits of the tree; on Rosh HaShanah, all the inhabitants of the world pass before Him like the flock of Beit Maron; and on the festival, they are judged concerning the water. And since Pesach is a day of judgment and all the judgments are yet to be carried out, therefore no rejoicing at all was mentioned for it. But for Atzeret, since one judgment — that of Pesach — has already passed, one rejoicing was written for it. And for the festival, since three judgments have already passed — Pesach, Atzeret, and Rosh HaShanah — therefore three rejoicings were written for it. End." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> These three pilgrimage festivals mentioned in the parashiyyot in their order — Pesach, Shavuot, Sukkot — correspond to the three patriarchs: Ḥesed, Din, and Raḥamim. Pesach corresponds to the attribute of Ḥesed, as it says (Exodus 12:23) ״ופסח ה׳ על הפתח״, and Onkelos translated it ״ואחוס״ — 'and I will spare,' and this is what is written (Psalms 136:10–11): ״למכה מצרים בבכוריהם כי לעולם חסדו״ — for the plague of the firstborn through which Israel went out was judgment for Egypt and Ḥesed for Israel. Shavuot corresponds to the attribute of Raḥamim, which is the decisive balance, and from this they interpreted (I Chronicles 29): ״והתפארת״ — this is the giving of the Torah, for the Torah was given in the special Name. Sukkot corresponds to the attribute of Din — the expression of סכך (sukkah) in the holy spirit (ru'ach ha-kodesh), and so they said: Why was she called Yiskah? Because she was סוכה ברוה״ק — a sukkah in the holy spirit. And this is (Psalms 68:18): ״אדני בם סיני בקדש״ — that is, in Wisdom, for the commandment of the sukkah alludes to Wisdom. Therefore they interpreted: ״קדש בסיני״ is not written, but rather ״סיני בקדש״ — this teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, is the place of His world, but His world is not His place. The meaning is that He is the place of His world, or the house of His world. And so Wisdom is alluded to in the bet (ב) of בראשית, and the sukkah is called a house, as it says (Genesis 33:17): ״ויעקב נסע סכותה ויבן לו בית״, and so it says (Proverbs 24:3): ״בחכמה יבנה בית״, and it is written (Proverbs 9:1): ״בית נתיבות נצבה״. And Wisdom encompasses and surrounds all, and its paradigm is the letter bet, which is enclosed on three sides, corresponding to the requirement of the sukkah, which is three walls — two according to their full measure and a third even one handbreadth. And they needed to say ״and a third even one handbreadth״, corresponding to the tail of the bet that protrudes behind it, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:58.856851350Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}